WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:29.279
Coll: The Taliban poses a threat to
the stability of Pakistan and Afghanistan

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:37.279
where constitutional governments find themselves
really in a war with the Taliban

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.540
over legitimacy and territory.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:46.100
The concern is not to allow the Taliban
to return to power at the point of a gun.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:54.199
Haass: This is a critical moment for
Afghanistan as the era in which the international

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:59.199
community has invested enormously and has
held a large presence is essentially coming

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:04.420
to an end. So the real question is how well
will the Afghans deal with the continuing

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:09.410
security challenge they're going to face from
the Taliban and indirectly from Pakistan,

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.160
which continues to provide a sanctuary.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.370
Markey: The biggest concern for the
United States and the international community

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:20.150
is that if the Taliban retakes significant
control of Afghanistan or assert themselves

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:24.970
inside of Pakistan, they will again provide
safe haven to international terrorists like

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:31.070
al Qaeda who attacked us on 9/11. Of course,
the humanitarian concerns are very real as

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:35.850
well. Wherever the Taliban have governed,
women have suffered. Children, especially

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:39.560
girls, haven't been able to go to school.
The kind of ruthless governance that they

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:44.030
provide is not satisfactory to any sense of
human rights.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.060
When I was in Swat,

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:51.120
which was a place
of tourism,

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:56.080
and beauty, suddenly changed into

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:03.400
a place of terrorism. I was just ten when
more than 400 schools were destroyed.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:09.280
Women were flogged. People were killed.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.140
And our
beautiful dreams turned into nightmares.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.400
Reid: A Talib can at its most anodyne
just mean student, religious student,

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:38.160
There is, of course, famously a much
darker side to this movement. They use an

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:45.420
interpretation of Sharia, of Islam, that is
very hardline, particularly around moral questions

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.060
like women's rights.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:58.720
Semple: The Afghan Taliban movement
originated in 1994 during the period of civil

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:05.740
war and chaos after the collapse of the Communist
government. They swept across the country

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:11.120
and during the rest of that decade, the Taliban
movement was essentially a governing party

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.740
that set up their version of an Islamic state
in Afghanistan.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:21.000
Markey: By 2001, it was clear that
the Afghan Taliban had a close relationship

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:26.890
with al Qaeda, and that's why after 9/11 the
United States invaded Afghanistan and deposed

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:28.470
the Taliban from Kabul.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:34.480
We will make no distinction between
the terrorists who committed these acts and

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:35.360
those who harbor them.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.960
Reid: After 9/11 the Taliban made a
resurgence in Afghanistan and have gradually

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:58.290
built themselves up to be quite an impressive
fighting force again. There are many reasons

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:03.170
why people are part of the Taliban movement.
You will see the hardcore religious ideologues

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:09.020
who believe that they're going to restore
Afghanistan to a proper Islamic state. You've

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:13.830
got some who just feel like power should be
theirs again. You've got some lower-level

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:18.349
guys who are probably just there for the money
or because they've had a local grievance in

00:04:18.349 --> 00:04:23.189
their village or district and the Taliban
are going to help them resolve it. And there

00:04:23.189 --> 00:04:28.349
are some who find it appealing; some of the
propaganda of the Taliban tends to be around

00:04:28.349 --> 00:04:30.549
forms of nationalism, anti-Westernism.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.560
Rashid: The Taliban leaders who fled
Afghanistan after 2001, they were given sanctuary

00:04:35.569 --> 00:04:41.979
in Pakistan, they were allowed to resuscitate
their movement in 2003, they were helped considerably

00:04:41.979 --> 00:04:44.069
by the Pakistani intelligence agencies.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:51.080
Siddique: Pakistan had a policy called
the ""strategic depth"" which called for dominating

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:58.890
Afghanistan through a client regime that will
not allow India into Afghanistan and will

00:04:59.629 --> 00:05:07.589
let Pakistan use Afghan territory in case
of a confrontation with India. Pakistan skillfully

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:14.620
channeled resources to nurture these extremely
violent Islamist factions among the Pashtuns,

00:05:15.099 --> 00:05:23.099
both in Pakistan and in Afghanistan. But eventually
some of them did turn against Pakistan.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:28.349
Coll: The Pakistani Taliban were born
in 2007, so much more recently than the Afghan

00:05:28.349 --> 00:05:34.439
Taliban. They are made up of Pakistani nationals,
and their goal is to carry out a revolution

00:05:34.439 --> 00:05:38.519
in Islamabad, the capital of Pakistan, not
in Kabul.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:19.080
Coll: While no one can imagine the
Taliban marching back into Kabul right away,

00:06:19.099 --> 00:06:24.930
as the United States withdraws and as NATO
withdraws it leaves behind Afghan security

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:31.930
forces that have lots of challenges, not just
their professional competency in the battlefield,

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:38.279
but also their cohesion politically and whether
they have a government that they can be loyal

00:06:38.279 --> 00:06:42.610
to, and whether they have enough national
support, popular support, whether they're

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:48.189
willing to kind of take the kind of casualties
that fighting the Taliban has required. So

00:06:48.189 --> 00:06:51.169
these are big questions about the future of
Afghanistan.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:56.599
We have to recognize that Afghanistan
will not be a perfect place, and it is not

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:02.839
America's responsibility to make it one.
The future of Afghanistan must be decided

00:07:02.839 --> 00:07:04.919
by Afghans.

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:13.029
Markey: As U.S. forces depart from
the region, the Afghan government and the

00:07:13.029 --> 00:07:18.479
other parties to this conflict face certain
clear dilemmas. The Afghan state needs to

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:23.249
come to terms with how much of the Afghan
insurgency can be brought into the political

00:07:23.249 --> 00:07:28.279
process, how much needs to be fought to a
standstill, how much needs to be destroyed

00:07:28.279 --> 00:07:30.779
in order for the Afghan state to survive.

00:07:30.779 --> 00:07:36.559
What the British public, the American
public, the public in all our partner countries

00:07:36.559 --> 00:07:43.539
Japan, European Union, everywhere else, Canada
expects is the same as what the Afghan public

00:07:43.539 --> 00:07:49.529
expects, a functioning government, a government
dedicated to the well-being of every Afghan.

00:07:49.529 --> 00:07:53.699
Markey: For its part, the Pakistani
state needs to determine how it's going to

00:07:53.699 --> 00:07:58.939
deal with its Pakistani Taliban insurgency.
It also needs to determine how it will deal

00:07:58.939 --> 00:08:04.439
with the Afghan Taliban, whether it will continue
to allow a safe haven to persist on its own

00:08:04.439 --> 00:08:10.569
soil. The Taliban insurgents also face a dilemma.
They need to determine what kind of political

00:08:10.569 --> 00:08:15.619
settlement they would be willing to accept,
whether from Islamabad or from Kabul, whether

00:08:15.619 --> 00:08:21.379
they could be brought into the future state
of Afghanistan or a Pakistani state, and what

00:08:21.379 --> 00:08:25.999
their main goals are moving forward. And because
it's not a monolith, different members of

00:08:25.999 --> 00:08:27.499
the Taliban are likely to choose differently.

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:31.600
Haass: The United States and the rest
of the world which has invested so much over

00:08:31.619 --> 00:08:36.279
the years in Afghanistan, has really got to
decide what it is they're prepared to live

00:08:36.279 --> 00:08:40.149
with and what matters most . There's one school
of thought that would say, we don't really

00:08:40.149 --> 00:08:45.290
care what Afghanistan looks like, so long
as the government that runs it, whether it's

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:49.670
some version of the current government, the
Taliban, or some combination, so long as they

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:54.300
don't allow their country to be used as a
terrorism base. Or the United States and others

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:59.900
have to say, we care about that but not just
that. What we also care about is the quality

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:01.460
and nature of life.

